  <title xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Regional Climate Resilience Program for Eastern and Southern Africa 2 Project (RCRP2)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This operation is the second operation of the Regional Climate Resilience Program for Eastern and Southern Africa Series of Projects (RCRP SOP), described in detailed in the Project Document of the first operation of the SOP, P180171. RCRP-1 includes Comoros, Madagascar, Mozambique, South Sudan, SADC, and ENTRO. RCRP-2 - this operation - includes Malawi and the African Union Commission (AUC). 

The overarching Development Objective of the Series of Projects (SOP) is to strengthen the resilience to water-related climate impacts  in Eastern and Southern African (AFE) countries . To achieve this overarching objective, the RCRP SOP supports investments to  enhance capacity building and infrastructure through progressive, overlapping projects over a period of ten years. The RCRP SOP provides a long-term instrument to address the  complex challenges climate change bears on water resources, and in turn, on AFE economies and people. The SOP design was chosen because it allows for tailored support to the needs of diverse countries, while supporting the achievement of a common long-term regional, overarching objective.

RCRP-2 activities are organized around five components, aligned to RCRP-1&apos;s components: (i) Risk Management and Climate Financing; (ii) Infrastructure Investments and Sustainable Asset Management for Climate Resilience; (iii) Adaptive Climate Services for Resilient Communities; and (iv) Project Management (v) CERC. The estimated envelope is $250 million. 

For Malawi, RCRP-2 will finance urgent reconstruction activities, and activities to increase the resilience to climate shocks in the medium to long term. To achieve the latter, it will advance a vision for natural resources management and flood risk management within the Shire Basin, in particular, in close coordination with Mozambique and SADC under RCRP-1. With the increased frequency and intensity of water-related disasters, this Project aims to disrupt the repeated focus on response, to advance a systemic change in the way disasters are anticipated, managed and avoided.  Resources from the IDA Crisis Response Window will finance the reconstruction of needed connectivity (roads, bridges, culverts), hydraulic and other critical infrastructure. For the AUC, the operation will focus on strengthening climate information systems for resilience and disaster risk management in the region, enhancing climate resilience in Africa&apos;s water systems, and strengthening resilience to water-related disasters; thus reinforcing and complementing the activities undertaken at the country level.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To improve resilience to water-related climate shocks in Malawi and in the Eastern and Southern Africa region, and in case of an Eligible Crisis or Emergency, to respond promptly and effectively to it.</narrative>
  </description>
